1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,085
On the night of December twenty-third,

2
00:00:02,105 --> 00:00:05,140
1944, twenty-five German prisoners of war

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:07,740
crawled out of a camp in the Arizona

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,665
desert through a tunnel they had dug by hand.

5
00:00:10,685 --> 00:00:13,265
Three of them took a boat with them,

6
00:00:13,285 --> 00:00:13,915
in pieces.

7
00:00:13,935 --> 00:00:16,210
They meant to paddle it to Mexico.

8
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:19,886
The boat had been built in secret,

9
00:00:19,906 --> 00:00:21,515
from scrap lumber and canvas.

10
00:00:21,535 --> 00:00:23,470
The route came from a map.

11
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:25,425
A canal to the Salt River,

12
00:00:25,445 --> 00:00:28,357
the Salt to the Gila, the Gila to the

13
00:00:28,377 --> 00:00:30,638
Colorado, and the Colorado to the sea.

14
00:00:30,658 --> 00:00:33,265
On paper, it was water all the way.

15
00:00:34,525 --> 00:00:36,825
On the ground, it was something else.

16
00:00:36,845 --> 00:00:39,145
This is the documented story of the

17
00:00:39,165 --> 00:00:41,465
largest escape of enemy prisoners from an

18
00:00:41,485 --> 00:00:43,785
American camp in the Second World War,

19
00:00:43,805 --> 00:00:46,125
and of the desert that caught them.

20
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,685
Camp Papago Park sat on the eastern edge

21
00:00:50,705 --> 00:00:52,902
of Phoenix, among red sandstone buttes.

22
00:00:52,922 --> 00:00:57,260
It opened in 1943, and by early 1944 it

23
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:00,220
held only Germans, most of them sailors of

24
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,330
the German navy.

25
00:01:01,350 --> 00:01:04,290
At its peak, it held about three thousand

26
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:06,510
one hundred prisoners, watched by three

27
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:08,010
hundred and seventy-one Americans.

28
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,692
Many of them were U-boat men,

29
00:01:11,712 --> 00:01:14,389
and the most stubborn were sent here.

30
00:01:14,409 --> 00:01:16,700
Their senior officer was Captain Jürgen

31
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:17,086
Wattenberg.

32
00:01:17,106 --> 00:01:19,783
He had been navigation officer of the

33
00:01:19,803 --> 00:01:20,938
warship Graf Spee.

34
00:01:20,958 --> 00:01:24,021
At forty-one, he took his own U-boat to

35
00:01:24,041 --> 00:01:25,947
sea and sank fourteen ships,

36
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:28,259
until three British destroyers sank him

37
00:01:28,279 --> 00:01:31,900
off Trinidad in September 1942.

38
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,032
The camp's commander would call him the

39
00:01:34,052 --> 00:01:35,880
number one super-Nazi of the camp.

40
00:01:37,175 --> 00:01:39,858
The camp had already seen what the

41
00:01:39,878 --> 00:01:41,017
hard-liners would do.

42
00:01:41,037 --> 00:01:44,754
On March twelfth, 1944, a sailor named

43
00:01:44,774 --> 00:01:45,932
Werner Drechsler arrived.

44
00:01:45,952 --> 00:01:48,682
He had been informing on other prisoners

45
00:01:48,702 --> 00:01:50,254
for American naval intelligence,

46
00:01:50,274 --> 00:01:52,611
and his old crewmates recognized him.

47
00:01:52,631 --> 00:01:54,575
Within hours, he was dead,

48
00:01:54,595 --> 00:01:56,932
found hanging in a shower room.

49
00:01:56,952 --> 00:02:00,095
Seven prisoners were later hanged for his murder.

50
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:04,181
For everyone else, life at Papago was easy.

51
00:02:04,201 --> 00:02:06,320
Officers did not have to work.

52
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:09,171
The prisoners called the camp the land of

53
00:02:09,191 --> 00:02:10,240
milk and honey.

54
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:12,022
But the officers had time,

55
00:02:12,042 --> 00:02:14,873
and they had a fence to look at.

56
00:02:14,893 --> 00:02:17,011
One of them, Lieutenant Wolfgang Clarus,

57
00:02:17,031 --> 00:02:19,506
said they saw three ways past it.

58
00:02:19,526 --> 00:02:21,644
Go through it, fly over it,

59
00:02:21,664 --> 00:02:23,090
or dig under it.

60
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:26,125
The camp's provost marshal,

61
00:02:26,145 --> 00:02:28,442
Captain Cecil Parshall, already knew where

62
00:02:28,462 --> 00:02:29,600
the weakness was.

63
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:32,303
Part of the officers' compound could not

64
00:02:32,323 --> 00:02:34,620
be seen from any guard tower.

65
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:37,323
"I knew they would discover that blind

66
00:02:37,343 --> 00:02:38,868
spot," he said later.

67
00:02:38,888 --> 00:02:42,155
In September 1944, four U-boat captains,

68
00:02:42,175 --> 00:02:43,813
planning over games of bridge,

69
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:45,160
started a tunnel there.

70
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:48,500
The entrance was in a bathhouse,

71
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,973
the building closest to the eastern fence.

72
00:02:50,993 --> 00:02:54,153
They loosened a board in the wall and hid

73
00:02:54,173 --> 00:02:56,627
the opening behind a large coal box.

74
00:02:56,647 --> 00:02:59,100
The Americans even lent them shovels and

75
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:00,180
rakes, for gardening.

76
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:04,264
They dug at night, in crews of three,

77
00:03:04,284 --> 00:03:05,972
in shifts of ninety minutes.

78
00:03:05,992 --> 00:03:09,389
One man dug, one hauled the dirt back in a

79
00:03:09,409 --> 00:03:11,097
bucket, and one kept watch.

80
00:03:11,117 --> 00:03:13,831
The tunnel was less than three feet across.

81
00:03:13,851 --> 00:03:16,906
To pass under a drainage ditch and a road,

82
00:03:16,926 --> 00:03:18,615
it dropped fourteen feet down.

83
00:03:18,635 --> 00:03:21,710
On a good night, it moved three feet forward.

84
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,600
The hardest problem was the dirt.

85
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:27,820
They flushed it down the toilets.

86
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,040
They hid it in the attics.

87
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:32,000
They carried it out in their pockets and

88
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,590
let it fall from their trouser legs.

89
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:37,810
Then one of the captains, Jürgen

90
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:39,660
Quaet-Faslem, had a better idea.

91
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,640
He asked the Americans for a sports field.

92
00:03:43,975 --> 00:03:46,569
The game was faustball, a German sport

93
00:03:46,589 --> 00:03:47,689
much like volleyball.

94
00:03:47,709 --> 00:03:49,183
The guards were delighted.

95
00:03:49,203 --> 00:03:52,171
It was the first time these prisoners had

96
00:03:52,191 --> 00:03:53,291
cooperated with anything.

97
00:03:53,311 --> 00:03:55,905
Every day, fresh earth was raked across

98
00:03:55,925 --> 00:03:58,892
the new court, and the Americans took it

99
00:03:58,912 --> 00:04:00,013
as German tidiness.

100
00:04:00,033 --> 00:04:02,253
In late November, an inspector stood

101
00:04:02,273 --> 00:04:05,241
almost on top of the entrance and declared

102
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:07,875
the ground as hard as a rock.

103
00:04:09,175 --> 00:04:11,732
On December twentieth, the tunnel was finished.

104
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:14,310
It ran one hundred and seventy-eight feet,

105
00:04:14,330 --> 00:04:16,519
from the bathhouse, under the fence,

106
00:04:16,539 --> 00:04:19,097
to the bank of the Crosscut Canal.

107
00:04:19,117 --> 00:04:22,042
The exit was hidden beneath a wooden box

108
00:04:22,062 --> 00:04:23,535
of dirt and grass.

109
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:27,693
They had been saving food for weeks.

110
00:04:27,713 --> 00:04:30,155
Bread was toasted, crushed into crumbs and

111
00:04:30,175 --> 00:04:32,618
wrapped in waxed paper from cereal boxes,

112
00:04:32,638 --> 00:04:36,136
to be mixed with water or milk on the road.

113
00:04:36,156 --> 00:04:37,895
Photographs were turned into forged

114
00:04:37,915 --> 00:04:40,006
papers, with stamps carved from leather

115
00:04:40,026 --> 00:04:40,826
and rubber.

116
00:04:42,025 --> 00:04:44,691
Three officers had a different plan from

117
00:04:44,711 --> 00:04:45,459
everyone else.

118
00:04:45,479 --> 00:04:47,377
Captain Wilhelm Günther and Lieutenants

119
00:04:47,397 --> 00:04:49,296
Wolfgang Clarus and Friedrich Utzolino

120
00:04:49,316 --> 00:04:51,983
built a folding boat from scavenged wood.

121
00:04:52,002 --> 00:04:55,053
They got canvas and tar by saying they

122
00:04:55,072 --> 00:04:56,971
needed to repair the roofs.

123
00:04:56,991 --> 00:04:59,658
No piece could be longer than eighteen

124
00:04:59,678 --> 00:05:02,344
inches, so the whole boat would fit

125
00:05:02,364 --> 00:05:03,495
through the tunnel.

126
00:05:03,515 --> 00:05:06,585
They became known as the three mad boatmen.

127
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:10,527
The date was Saturday, December twenty-third.

128
00:05:10,547 --> 00:05:13,207
In the next compound, prisoners threw a

129
00:05:13,227 --> 00:05:15,504
loud party to cover any noise.

130
00:05:15,524 --> 00:05:18,567
At nine o'clock, the first men went down

131
00:05:18,587 --> 00:05:19,333
the shaft.

132
00:05:19,353 --> 00:05:21,630
By two thirty in the morning,

133
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:23,927
all twenty-five were out, twelve officers

134
00:05:23,947 --> 00:05:25,459
and thirteen enlisted men.

135
00:05:25,479 --> 00:05:28,521
They waded through the icy water of the

136
00:05:28,541 --> 00:05:32,350
canal in the rain, and split up into small groups.

137
00:05:32,370 --> 00:05:34,264
Most of them headed south.

138
00:05:34,284 --> 00:05:36,944
Mexico was about one hundred and thirty

139
00:05:36,964 --> 00:05:37,764
miles away.

140
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:41,631
They spent Christmas Eve on the run.

141
00:05:41,651 --> 00:05:43,882
One group rested in a stable,

142
00:05:43,902 --> 00:05:46,133
eating bread crumbs and canned milk,

143
00:05:46,153 --> 00:05:48,759
while a Mexican family nearby sang carols.

144
00:05:48,779 --> 00:05:51,760
In another group, a man with a harmonica

145
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,905
played Silent Night.

146
00:05:54,225 --> 00:05:57,007
Back at the camp, nobody noticed for most

147
00:05:57,027 --> 00:05:58,058
of a day.

148
00:05:58,078 --> 00:06:00,159
The German officers stalled the Sunday

149
00:06:00,179 --> 00:06:02,261
count by demanding that an American

150
00:06:02,281 --> 00:06:03,312
officer take it.

151
00:06:03,332 --> 00:06:05,063
By about seven that evening,

152
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:06,814
Parshall knew men were gone,

153
00:06:06,834 --> 00:06:09,625
and called the FBI.

154
00:06:09,645 --> 00:06:12,047
Hundreds of soldiers, FBI agents and

155
00:06:12,067 --> 00:06:13,900
Papago trackers joined the search,

156
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:16,867
with a reward of twenty-five dollars a man.

157
00:06:16,887 --> 00:06:19,462
The Phoenix Gazette called it the greatest

158
00:06:19,482 --> 00:06:20,965
manhunt in Arizona history.

159
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:25,333
The desert did most of the work.

160
00:06:25,353 --> 00:06:27,935
The nights were cold, it kept raining,

161
00:06:27,955 --> 00:06:30,538
and the men were hungry and lost.

162
00:06:30,558 --> 00:06:32,396
A twenty-two-year-old U-boat sailor named

163
00:06:32,416 --> 00:06:34,627
Herbert Fuchs walked into the Phoenix

164
00:06:34,647 --> 00:06:36,858
sheriff's office and gave himself up.

165
00:06:36,878 --> 00:06:38,717
Two more surrendered in Tempe.

166
00:06:38,737 --> 00:06:42,063
One stepped on a cactus and limped to a

167
00:06:42,083 --> 00:06:43,550
farmhouse with his partner,

168
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:46,152
where the mother of the family later

169
00:06:46,172 --> 00:06:48,775
remembered them as charming and handsome guests.

170
00:06:49,475 --> 00:06:52,319
The people of Phoenix were angrier about

171
00:06:52,339 --> 00:06:55,182
what the prisoners carried than about the

172
00:06:55,202 --> 00:06:56,000
escape itself.

173
00:06:56,020 --> 00:06:57,637
Some carried rationed goods.

174
00:06:57,657 --> 00:06:59,682
One resident complained that citizens

175
00:06:59,702 --> 00:07:03,364
could not get a single slice of bacon for

176
00:07:03,384 --> 00:07:04,591
weeks on end.

177
00:07:04,611 --> 00:07:07,475
Nobody was attacked, and nothing was sabotaged.

178
00:07:08,725 --> 00:07:11,118
The best of them came close.

179
00:07:11,138 --> 00:07:14,336
On New Year's Day, a Papago tracker found

180
00:07:14,356 --> 00:07:17,553
two of them asleep less than thirty miles

181
00:07:17,573 --> 00:07:18,760
from the border.

182
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:21,173
On January sixth, Indian scouts caught

183
00:07:21,193 --> 00:07:23,184
Captain Quaet-Faslem and Captain Friedrich

184
00:07:23,204 --> 00:07:25,597
Guggenberger within ten miles of Mexico.

185
00:07:25,617 --> 00:07:27,608
Three years earlier, Guggenberger's U-boat

186
00:07:27,628 --> 00:07:30,845
had sunk the British aircraft carrier Ark Royal.

187
00:07:32,575 --> 00:07:34,999
The boatmen never got their boat wet.

188
00:07:35,019 --> 00:07:37,793
The Salt River, the first blue line on

189
00:07:37,813 --> 00:07:40,587
their map, had almost no water in it.

190
00:07:40,607 --> 00:07:42,333
The rain shrank their canvas.

191
00:07:42,353 --> 00:07:44,079
When they reached the Gila,

192
00:07:44,099 --> 00:07:46,872
it was too shallow and muddy to float

193
00:07:46,892 --> 00:07:47,571
their boat.

194
00:07:47,591 --> 00:07:50,035
They left the boat behind and walked.

195
00:07:51,325 --> 00:07:53,964
On January eighth, near the town of Gila

196
00:07:53,984 --> 00:07:56,622
Bend, one of them washed his underwear and

197
00:07:56,642 --> 00:07:59,281
hung it on a mesquite tree to dry.

198
00:07:59,301 --> 00:08:01,608
Cowboys saw it and called the authorities.

199
00:08:01,628 --> 00:08:02,937
Clarus put it simply.

200
00:08:02,957 --> 00:08:05,596
We should have known that the Gila wasn't

201
00:08:05,616 --> 00:08:06,945
much of a river.

202
00:08:08,675 --> 00:08:10,027
Wattenberg never went south.

203
00:08:10,047 --> 00:08:12,427
With two sailors, Walter Kozur and Johann

204
00:08:12,447 --> 00:08:15,514
Kremer, he hid in a cave in the mountains

205
00:08:15,534 --> 00:08:16,886
north of the city.

206
00:08:16,906 --> 00:08:19,286
Every few days, Kremer traded places with

207
00:08:19,306 --> 00:08:21,687
a prisoner on an outside work detail.

208
00:08:21,707 --> 00:08:24,431
Kremer would walk back into the camp to

209
00:08:24,451 --> 00:08:27,174
collect food and news, and the other man

210
00:08:27,194 --> 00:08:29,595
would spend the night in the cave.

211
00:08:30,950 --> 00:08:32,763
The trick worked for weeks.

212
00:08:32,783 --> 00:08:34,963
Then, late in January, a surprise

213
00:08:34,983 --> 00:08:37,163
inspection found Kremer inside the camp.

214
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:39,363
The next night, three soldiers were

215
00:08:39,383 --> 00:08:41,930
waiting at an abandoned car where the

216
00:08:41,950 --> 00:08:44,130
supplies were hidden, and Kozur walked

217
00:08:44,150 --> 00:08:45,250
straight into them.

218
00:08:46,475 --> 00:08:47,500
On January twenty-seventh,

219
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:49,590
Wattenberg cleaned himself up and walked

220
00:08:49,610 --> 00:08:51,681
into Phoenix with seventy-five cents in

221
00:08:51,701 --> 00:08:52,377
his pocket.

222
00:08:52,397 --> 00:08:55,164
He spent most of it on a meal,

223
00:08:55,184 --> 00:08:57,951
slept in a chair in a hotel lobby,

224
00:08:57,971 --> 00:09:00,042
and walked the streets at night.

225
00:09:00,062 --> 00:09:02,829
Then he asked a street cleaner for directions.

226
00:09:02,849 --> 00:09:05,964
The man did not like his accent and called

227
00:09:05,984 --> 00:09:06,661
the police.

228
00:09:06,681 --> 00:09:09,100
By nine the next morning, Wattenberg was

229
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:11,190
back at Papago Park, thirty-five days

230
00:09:11,210 --> 00:09:12,255
after he left.

231
00:09:13,975 --> 00:09:17,260
Some of the escapees expected to be shot.

232
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:20,152
Instead, each man got bread and water,

233
00:09:20,172 --> 00:09:23,870
one day for every day he had been gone.

234
00:09:23,890 --> 00:09:26,762
No American officer or guard was court-martialed.

235
00:09:26,782 --> 00:09:29,327
The FBI looked into how loosely Arizona's

236
00:09:29,347 --> 00:09:30,435
camps were run.

237
00:09:31,725 --> 00:09:34,272
Clarus later said the tunnel had kept

238
00:09:34,292 --> 00:09:36,838
their spirits up, even as Germany was

239
00:09:36,858 --> 00:09:37,572
being crushed.

240
00:09:37,592 --> 00:09:40,549
In May 1945, Germany surrendered.

241
00:09:40,569 --> 00:09:43,013
After the war, Guggenberger joined the new

242
00:09:43,033 --> 00:09:45,125
West German navy, rose to admiral,

243
00:09:45,145 --> 00:09:48,665
and served as a deputy chief of staff at NATO.

244
00:09:50,050 --> 00:09:52,798
Wattenberg came back to Arizona as an old

245
00:09:52,818 --> 00:09:55,220
man, for reunions with his former guards.

246
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:59,620
At one of them, in 1985, he said it was

247
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:02,081
strange that men had to kill each other

248
00:10:02,101 --> 00:10:03,312
before they became friends.

249
00:10:03,332 --> 00:10:05,710
He died in Hamburg in 1995,

250
00:10:05,730 --> 00:10:07,475
at ninety-four.

251
00:10:08,175 --> 00:10:10,054
If someone in your family lived near

252
00:10:10,074 --> 00:10:12,225
Phoenix during the war, or guarded a camp

253
00:10:12,245 --> 00:10:14,415
like this one, tell us in the comments.

254
00:10:16,175 --> 00:10:19,136
A plaque on the bank of the Crosscut Canal

255
00:10:19,156 --> 00:10:21,787
marks the spot where the tunnel came up.

256
00:10:21,807 --> 00:10:23,774
Twenty-five men dug one hundred and

257
00:10:23,794 --> 00:10:25,762
seventy-eight feet under the desert to

258
00:10:25,782 --> 00:10:26,756
reach a river.

259
00:10:26,776 --> 00:10:28,412
They planned for the guards,

260
00:10:28,432 --> 00:10:30,068
the fence and the dirt.

261
00:10:30,088 --> 00:10:32,719
What they could not see from inside the

262
00:10:32,739 --> 00:10:34,395
wire was the river itself.

